From 35681a3fa0230fec4ede0f8a4f0a9edab624685c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeepSeek Harness <noreply@deepseek.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 03:23:42 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 17/18] Point the updater at the Windows releases, and
 implement it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two separate things were pointing at the macOS repository.

The core's update entry points were honest failures; they now run a real
updater against api.github.com/repos/greyoak111/livehime-win. Verified the way
that can actually be verified, by asking it: an auto-check fifteen seconds after
launch reports

  [livehime] update -> checking
  [livehime] update -> failed reason: network
  [livehime] update failed: network HTTP 404

A 404 is the evidence. livehime-win has no releases yet, so /releases/latest
404s; had it still been asking livehime-macos it would have found releases and
answered upToDate or available instead. The per-platform releases link in
update-page.cpp is fixed too — it was the fallback for "open the releases page",
so with automatic updates off a Windows user was being sent to the macOS
download.

What the updater is: feed parsing, a streaming downloader (net::Send buffers a
whole body, so the archive is streamed with 64 KiB WinHTTP reads straight to
disk and through an incremental SHA-256 on a worker thread), an in-file zip
reader over the already-linked zlib (Zip64, CRC per entry, refuses traversal,
absolute names, ADS, device names, encrypted and bzip2 entries), and an
install.cmd helper that outlives the process because Windows will not let a
running obs64.exe be overwritten. The swap is two renames inside one directory,
so it is same-volume by construction, with the old tree kept as
<root>.livehime-previous for rollback. OBS portable mode is refused before the
download, because its scenes live inside the tree an update would replace.

Publisher verification is absent and not faked: nothing on Windows is signed
yet and a zip carries no Authenticode signature, so SHA-256 plus the staged
executable's version resource and PE machine type is as far as this can honestly
go.

Two build fixes along the way: updater.cpp used INTERNET_PORT, URL_COMPONENTS,
WinHttpCrackUrl and HINTERNET without including <winhttp.h> (its author's clang
check passed against a hand-written stub, which is exactly the gap a stub
leaves), and EmitUnsupportedEvent was dead code that MSVC C4505 would fail a /WX
build on.

The update event now logs its status and reason, for the same reason danmaku
does: "up to date" without saying where it looked cannot be told apart from
having quietly asked the wrong repository.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
